"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.3, and the surgical findings indicated a pT3b stage with positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and lymph node metastasis. The biopsy findings showed mixed grades, with one lesion at Gleason 4+3 and another at Gleason 3+5. The presence of lymph node metastasis is a significant negative prognostic factor.\n\nCountervailing findings include the ISUP grade group 2 for the prostatectomy and the presence of lymphovascular invasion. The nodal status was documented as positive metastasis. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, with the prostatectomy grade being ISUP group 2, despite biopsy findings suggesting higher grades. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data widens the uncertainty regarding future trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 31.78 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 25.42–38.13 months; its coverage has not been validated."
